{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-13_10-13-13-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nupd={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Successor experiment for the wedge investigation that executes the second source-derived repair path, closing the last limitation recorded for this finding (the snapshot route was asserted from source but never run). The harness keeps the earlier subtests and adds a mode with tight compaction settings (snapshot threshold 1, trailing logs 0, snapshot interval an hour so nothing snapshots by itself) where, after the wedge and after the fault is removed, the leader takes a snapshot and a new client entry is produced, then the wedged node is sampled for four seconds. Native local feedback in a scratch copy inside draft showed the node's store being compacted and its certificate jumping to the new entry, but the retained execution decides.\\\",\n \\\"experiment\\\":{\n  \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"0bfc2ea5a9e94316982943d306eefbf6-question-0\\\",\n  \\\"harness_path\\\":\\\"assurance_generated_test.go\\\",\n  \\\"previous_id\\\":\\\"adeee037edde49b8b2eb64b8278a2187-experiment\\\",\n  \\\"revision_reason\\\":\\\"Same investigation and staging, extended with a subtest that repairs the wedged node through the snapshot route instead of a restart: aggressive compaction settings, a leader-triggered snapshot after the wedge, and a new entry produced afterwards so the observation covers replication resuming and not just state consistency.\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"appendentries-conflict-truncate-block\\\",\\\"appendentries-store-failure-todo\\\",\\\"appendentries-entry-loop-cached-lastlog\\\",\\\"replication-nextindex-floor\\\",\\\"inmem-store-getlog-missing\\\",\\\"state-getlastentry\\\",\\\"lastindex-public-api\\\"],\n  \\\"scope\\\":{\n   \\\"description\\\":\\\"Subtests: three-voter control, three-voter injected_store_error, injected_store_error_then_restart, control_five_voters and injected_store_error_five_voters (one or two escalation steps), and the new injected_store_error_then_snapshot. The snapshot subtest builds the same three-voter cluster with the fault wrapper, configures SnapshotThreshold 1, TrailingLogs 0 and a one-hour SnapshotInterval, runs the same partition/divergence/higher-term replacement history with the injected StoreLogs error, confirms the wedge, removes the fault, has the leader take a snapshot, applies one client entry, and then samples the wedged node's certificate, store first/last index, commit index and store counters every 200 ms for four seconds, together with the leader's probe, the store's first index before and after the snapshot and the snapshot future's error.\\\",\n   \\\"assumptions\\\":[\n    \\\"With TrailingLogs 0 the leader compacts its log up to the snapshot index, so its replication to a peer whose next index is pinned at the beginning can no longer read the entries and must send a snapshot, which is the library's own ErrLogNotFound to SEND_SNAP path.\\\",\n    \\\"The snapshot is triggered through the public Snapshot API on a leader whose FSM has applied state (MockFSM), so the snapshot content is the applied state at that moment.\\\",\n    \\\"The new client entry produced after the snapshot is what shows whether replication resumes past the install rather than only that the installed state is consistent.\\\",\n    \\\"A LogStore returning an error from StoreLogs is an allowed outcome of the interface; the wrapper returns it without touching the underlying store, and the fault is not re-armed during the snapshot phase.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"excluded\\\":[\n    \\\"No production code is modified; compaction is configured through Config and snapshots are taken through the public API.\\\",\n    \\\"No restart is performed in this subtest; the restart path is covered by its own receipt.\\\"\n   ]\n  },\n  \\\"prefix\\\":\\\"The snapshot subtest prints truncation_before_heal, truncation_after_heal and truncation_after_disarm like the other cases, then truncation_after_snapshot with snapshot_error, store_first_before, store_first_after, cert_samples, store_last_samples, store_first_samples, commit_samples (200 ms interval over four seconds), the wedged and leader probes, and elapsed_ms.\\\"\n }\n}\nopen('update.json','w').write(json.dumps(upd,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nprint('ok', json.load(open('update.json'))['experiment']['previous_id'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-13_10-13-13-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nupd={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Successor experiment for the wedge investigation that executes the second source-derived repair path, closing the last limitation recorded for this finding (the snapshot route was asserted from source but never run). The harness keeps the earlier subtests and adds a mode with tight compaction settings (snapshot threshold 1, trailing logs 0, snapshot interval an hour so nothing snapshots by itself) where, after the wedge and after the fault is removed, the leader takes a snapshot and a new client entry is produced, then the wedged node is sampled for four seconds. Native local feedback in a scratch copy inside draft showed the node's store being compacted and its certificate jumping to the new entry, but the retained execution decides.\\\",\n \\\"experiment\\\":{\n  \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"0bfc2ea5a9e94316982943d306eefbf6-question-0\\\",\n  \\\"harness_path\\\":\\\"assurance_generated_test.go\\\",\n  \\\"previous_id\\\":\\\"adeee037edde49b8b2eb64b8278a2187-experiment\\\",\n  \\\"revision_reason\\\":\\\"Same investigation and staging, extended with a subtest that repairs the wedged node through the snapshot route instead of a restart: aggressive compaction settings, a leader-triggered snapshot after the wedge, and a new entry produced afterwards so the observation covers replication resuming and not just state consistency.\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"appendentries-conflict-truncate-block\\\",\\\"appendentries-store-failure-todo\\\",\\\"appendentries-entry-loop-cached-lastlog\\\",\\\"replication-nextindex-floor\\\",\\\"inmem-store-getlog-missing\\\",\\\"state-getlastentry\\\",\\\"lastindex-public-api\\\"],\n  \\\"scope\\\":{\n   \\\"description\\\":\\\"Subtests: three-voter control, three-voter injected_store_error, injected_store_error_then_restart, control_five_voters and injected_store_error_five_voters (one or two escalation steps), and the new injected_store_error_then_snapshot. The snapshot subtest builds the same three-voter cluster with the fault wrapper, configures SnapshotThreshold 1, TrailingLogs 0 and a one-hour SnapshotInterval, runs the same partition/divergence/higher-term replacement history with the injected StoreLogs error, confirms the wedge, removes the fault, has the leader take a snapshot, applies one client entry, and then samples the wedged node's certificate, store first/last index, commit index and store counters every 200 ms for four seconds, together with the leader's probe, the store's first index before and after the snapshot and the snapshot future's error.\\\",\n   \\\"assumptions\\\":[\n    \\\"With TrailingLogs 0 the leader compacts its log up to the snapshot index, so its replication to a peer whose next index is pinned at the beginning can no longer read the entries and must send a snapshot, which is the library's own ErrLogNotFound to SEND_SNAP path.\\\",\n    \\\"The snapshot is triggered through the public Snapshot API on a leader whose FSM has applied state (MockFSM), so the snapshot content is the applied state at that moment.\\\",\n    \\\"The new client entry produced after the snapshot is what shows whether replication resumes past the install rather than only that the installed state is consistent.\\\",\n    \\\"A LogStore returning an error from StoreLogs is an allowed outcome of the interface; the wrapper returns it without touching the underlying store, and the fault is not re-armed during the snapshot phase.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"excluded\\\":[\n    \\\"No production code is modified; compaction is configured through Config and snapshots are taken through the public API.\\\",\n    \\\"No restart is performed in this subtest; the restart path is covered by its own receipt.\\\"\n   ]\n  },\n  \\\"prefix\\\":\\\"The snapshot subtest prints truncation_before_heal, truncation_after_heal and truncation_after_disarm like the other cases, then truncation_after_snapshot with snapshot_error, store_first_before, store_first_after, cert_samples, store_last_samples, store_first_samples, commit_samples (200 ms interval over four seconds), the wedged and leader probes, and elapsed_ms.\\\"\n }\n}\nopen('update.json','w').write(json.dumps(upd,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nprint('ok', json.load(open('update.json'))['experiment']['previous_id'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"ok adeee037edde49b8b2eb64b8278a2187-experiment\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
